---
/**
 * The pre-season empty state.
 *
 * This is the honest answer to "what are the standings?" — there aren't any, and
 * the page says so first, in plain words, before explaining anything else. It
 * also names the specific things missing, because "coming soon" tells a club
 * nothing about what would have to happen for their name to appear here.
 */
import { K, MIN_ROSTER } from '../lib/scoring/index.js';

interface Props {
  reasons: {
    noSeason: boolean;
    noEnrollmentData: boolean;
    noVerifiedRosters: boolean;
  };
}
const { reasons } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="pre">
  <h2 class="pre__head">The first NCBO season hasn't started yet.</h2>
  <p class="pre__lead">
    There are no standings, because no club has competed for points. Here's how standing will be
    scored when the season does start — the system is built and running, it just has nothing to
    rank.
  </p>

  <h3>What has to exist before this table fills in</h3>
  <ul class="pre__list">
    {
      reasons.noSeason && (
        <li>
          <strong>A season.</strong> Sanctioned events with a points weight, and results recorded
          against them. Nothing is scheduled yet.
        </li>
      )
    }
    {
      reasons.noEnrollmentData && (
        <li>
          <strong>Enrollment denominators.</strong> Standing is a rate, not a total, so every ranked
          club needs a verified undergraduate enrollment figure. These come from IPEDS, the US
          Department of Education's public data set. The fetch has not been run, and no figure is
          estimated in the meantime.
        </li>
      )
    }
    {
      reasons.noVerifiedRosters && (
        <li>
          <strong>Verified rosters.</strong> A club needs at least {MIN_ROSTER} verified members to be
          ranked at all. Below that, a rate is mostly noise, so clubs are listed as "building
          roster" instead of being given a position they haven't earned.
        </li>
      )
    }
  </ul>

  <h3>The short version of the scoring</h3>
  <p>
    Clubs are ranked on points per 1,000 undergraduates, not on raw points — otherwise the biggest
    schools win by default. But a plain per-capita rate has the opposite problem: a small
    denominator inflates the quotient, so the smallest schools would win by default instead, and one
    good week from a twelve-person club would swing the national table.
  </p>
  <p>
    So each club's rate is <strong>shrunk toward the league mean</strong> by an amount that depends on
    how much enrollment stands behind it (K = {K}). Big clubs barely move. Very small ones move a
    long way, because there is genuinely less evidence about them. Both the raw and the adjusted rate
    are shown, always.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="pre__link" href="/how-standing-works">Read the full explanation, including its limits</a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .pre {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--steel);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    padding: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2rem);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }
  .pre__head {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.9rem);
  }
  .pre__lead {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  .pre h3 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
  .pre__list {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .pre__list li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .pre p {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  .pre__link {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
</style>
